    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The pathogen Salmonella enterica serovar Typhimurium encodes a tightly regulated Type 6 Secretion System (T6SS) which plays a large role in interbacterial competition. In an effort to further understand how this secretion system is regulated, our lab recently conducted a transposon screen to identify regulators of expression of T6SS genes. Transposon insertions immediately upstream of TomB, the Hha antitoxin, led to increased T6SS expression. Hha is a conditional toxin that interacts with Histone-like Nucleoid Structuring (HNS) proteins which have been previously identified as repressors of Salmonella’s T6SS. Previous studies have shown that a strain deficient in Hha and its paralog, Ydg-T, increases expression of several Salmonella Pathogenicity Island 6 (SPI-6) genes. I hypothesized TomB plays a role in regulating Salmonella’s T6SS. In this thesis, I report that the overexpression of TomB using an arabinose inducible plasmid increases expression of T6SS proteins such as ClpV, Hcp1, and Tae4. Furthermore, through in vitro competition assays, I show evidence of increased T6SS-dependent killing of prey in strains of S. Typhimurium that overexpress TomB.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
